context='First-line extensive-stage small-cell lung cancer'
schedule='Induction: atezolizumab and carboplatin on Day 1, with etoposide on Days 1–3, repeated every 21 days for up to four cycles. Maintenance: carboplatin and etoposide stop; atezolizumab alone continues every 21 days while appropriate. The fourth cycle ends about 12 weeks after starting if there are no delays; maintenance has no fixed total duration in this regimen.'
routes='Confirm your written route plan. Atezolizumab may be a drip (IV) or an injection under the skin of the thigh (SC). Etoposide may be IV on Days 1–3, or IV on Day 1 with prescribed capsules twice daily on Days 2–3. These schedules use different doses; do not change route or convert doses yourself. A separately prescribed four-week IV atezolizumab maintenance plan uses NCCP 00593 and is outside this 21-day calendar.'
admin='Day 1 order: atezolizumab, carboplatin, then etoposide. Atezolizumab IV first infusion is 60 minutes, later 30 minutes if tolerated; SC injection takes about 7 minutes. Carboplatin infusion is 30 minutes and etoposide IV is 60 minutes. Total unit time also includes checks, preparation and observation. SC atezolizumab does not remove the chemotherapy drips during induction.'
baseline='Confirm adult first-line ES-SCLC, ECOG 0–1 and adequate marrow/organ function; review symptoms, medicines and pregnancy possibility. FBC, renal/liver profile, glucose, thyroid function, hepatitis B (HBsAg and HBcoreAb) and hepatitis C screening. Measured isotope GFR preferred; otherwise verify a suitable estimate. Record baseline breathing, bowel, skin and endocrine symptoms.'
ongoing='FBC, liver, renal and glucose profile before every cycle; thyroid tests every 3–6 weeks. Scans and other disease assessment follow the consultant plan. Continue symptom review during maintenance and after atezolizumab stops because immune effects may be delayed.'
qual='In a randomised trial in previously untreated extensive-stage small-cell lung cancer, adding atezolizumab to carboplatin and IV etoposide improved survival and delayed progression on average. This cannot predict one person’s benefit.'
lim='IMpower133 studied IV atezolizumab with IV etoposide. It did not directly randomise the SC formulation or the Day 2–3 oral etoposide alternative; those are separate NCCP-listed schedules. Trial averages are not an individual prognosis or a comparison with another immunotherapy.'
