p['agents']=[agent('atezolizumab','Atezolizumab','Tecentriq','Immunotherapy','Blocks the PD-L1 immune brake','Atezolizumab is an antibody against PD-L1, a signal that can reduce an immune cell’s activity. Blocking that signal can help immune cells attack cancer. It works differently from chemotherapy. The same immune activation can inflame healthy organs, so new symptoms need assessment even when they look like ordinary treatment tiredness or a minor infection. IV and SC formulations use different doses but both carry immune risks.','Report new cough, breathlessness, diarrhoea, rash, severe headache, weakness, thirst, frequent urination or chest symptoms promptly. Effects can appear during treatment or after the final dose.',['Immune inflammation of lungs, bowel, liver, kidneys, hormone glands, skin, heart, muscles or nerves','Serious infusion or SC-related reactions','Some effects require prolonged treatment or lasting hormone replacement'],['Breathing / cough','Diarrhoea','Skin / nail changes','Fatigue'],['Immune cell','PD-L1 blocked','Cancer cell']),agent('carboplatin','Carboplatin','Platinum chemotherapy','Chemotherapy','Damages cancer-cell DNA','Carboplatin forms links in DNA that interfere with copying and repair. This helps stop dividing cancer cells but also affects healthy tissues such as bone marrow. Kidney function is used to calculate its dose. It is given during the four-cycle induction phase and stops before atezolizumab-only maintenance.','Report fever, shivering, unexpected bruising, bleeding or persistent vomiting. Tell the nurse immediately about flushing, itching, chest tightness, breathing changes or pain around the drip.',['Low blood counts, infection, anaemia and bleeding','Nausea, vomiting and fatigue','Allergic reactions; renal function affects dosing'],['Nausea / vomiting','Fatigue','Breathing / cough'],['DNA strands','Platinum links','Copying disrupted']),agent('etoposide','Etoposide','Topoisomerase II chemotherapy','Chemotherapy','Interferes with DNA repair','Etoposide interferes with topoisomerase II, an enzyme that helps manage DNA during copying. DNA damage can then accumulate and stop cell division. Healthy rapidly renewing tissues can also be affected, explaining low blood counts, hair loss and mouth or bowel problems. Oral absorption differs from IV delivery; the prescribed oral dose must not be inferred from the IV dose.','During an infusion, report dizziness, breathing changes, rash or pain at the drip. At home report fever, mouth ulcers, diarrhoea or poor intake. If capsules are prescribed, follow the exact labelled doses and contact the team about a missed or vomited dose rather than replacing it yourself.',['Low blood counts and infection','Hair loss, mouth soreness, nausea and bowel changes','Infusion reactions or low blood pressure during administration','Rare later treatment-related blood disorders'],['Mouth soreness / ulcers','Nausea / vomiting','Diarrhoea','Fatigue'],['DNA copying','Repair interrupted','Cell division slows'])]
